docs\journeys\scratch\mcp-server-audit.md:11:The proposed bridge RPC methods are not implemented in this MCP server. There is no `load_save`, no `capture_screenshot` bridge method, and no `query_telemetry` method anywhere under `Tools/wsm3d-mcp/` (`server.py:63-292`; `tools/game.py:65-224`; `tools/settings.py:10-95`; `tools/journey.py:11-92`; `tools/build.py:47-115`; `tools/codex.py:100-153`).
docs\journeys\scratch\mcp-server-audit.md:15:- `game_screenshot` captures the host desktop via PowerShell `CopyFromScreen`, so it is not an in-game bridge screenshot path (`tools/game.py:149-195`).
docs\journeys\scratch\mcp-server-audit.md:19:The design doc for plate-19 explicitly expects `load_save(path)`, `capture_screenshot(path)`, and `query_telemetry()` as bridge RPCs, plus a Unity-side main-thread queue (`docs/journeys/scratch/game-bridge-rpc-design.md:19-67`).
docs\journeys\scratch\mcp-server-audit.md:25:That means thread-safety concerns are currently host-side only. The proposed bridge design is different: Unity-safe work must be enqueued to a main-thread command queue drained from `Update()`; the HTTP request thread must not call Unity APIs directly (`docs/journeys/scratch/game-bridge-rpc-design.md:50-67`).
